  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify the first term \\(a\\) and the common ratio \\(r\\) of the geometric sequence.\nThe first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{4}\\) and the common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nStep 2: Use the formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric sequence, which is given by \\(S_n = a \\frac{1-r^n}{1-r}\\).\nSubstitute \\(a = \\frac{1}{4}\\), \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\), and \\(S_n = \\frac{255}{512}\\) into the formula:\n\\[\n\\frac{255}{512} = \\frac{1}{4} \\frac{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify the right side of the equation.\n\\[\n\\frac{255}{512} = \\frac{1}{4} \\frac{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right)\n\\]\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{255}{512} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right)\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Solve for \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\).\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\\[\n\\frac{255}{256} = 1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\n\\]\nRearrange to isolate \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n = 1 - \\frac{255}{256} = \\frac{1}{256}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^8 = \\frac{1}{256}\\), we have:\n\\[\nn = 8\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{8}<|im_end|>",
